Write $t=\mathrm{C(CH_3)_3}$. **D is the <sodium> <enolate> $\mathrm{t{-}C(O^-){=}CH_2\ Na^+}$.** Its two resonances in the <alkene>-<proton> region belong to the two <hydrogens> of the terminal <methylene group>. One is cis to <oxygen> and the other trans to <oxygen>, so they are not related by a <symmetry (physics)> operation even though both belong to the same <carbon>. Replacing one or the other gives different alkene geometries, so they are <diastereotopic>. Their weak or unresolved <geminal coupling> is consistent with the reported singlet descriptions. Without an additional spatial assignment, it is not justified to decide uniquely which numerical shift belongs to which of those two orientations. The nine-<proton> singlet is the <tert-butyl group>. The formula $\mathrm{C_6H_{11}ONa}$ follows from removing one alpha <hydrogen> from <pinacolone>.

**E is the <enol>-dominated <1,3-diketone> $\mathrm{tCO{-}CH_2{-}COt}$, observed as $\mathrm{tC(OH){=}CH{-}COt}$.** The very downfield broad signal belongs to the <enol> OH in a six-membered intramolecular O–H···O chelate. The one-<proton> signal in the vinyl region is its central <enol> CH; a predominantly diketo structure would instead require a two-<proton> central CH2 signal and no <enol> OH. The eighteen-<proton> singlet accounts for both <tert-butyl groups>. Rapid exchange between the two degenerate <enol> orientations can average their different local environments; accidental unresolved equality cannot be excluded by the supplied spectrum alone. Thus one should not infer a permanently symmetric single <enol> <Lewis structure> from that singlet. The total integration is $1+1+18=20$, agreeing with **$\mathrm{C_{11}H_{20}O_2}$**.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<amide ion> removes a methyl-side alpha <proton> of <pinacolone>. The C–H <electrons> form the <enolate> C=C bond as the <carbonyl> pi pair moves onto <oxygen>; <ammonia> is formed. The tert-butyl-side alpha <carbon> has no <hydrogen>, fixing the ordinary <enolate> regiochemistry.

The <carbon> end of D attacks the <acyl chloride> <carbonyl> <carbon>. Move the C=O pi pair onto <oxygen>, then collapse the <tetrahedral intermediate> with <chloride> departure. This <nucleophilic acyl substitution> gives $\mathrm{tCO{-}CH_2{-}COt}$ and constructs the bond between the original <enolate> methylene and the incoming <carbonyl>. **The observed product requires <carbon> acylation, rather than an O-acyl <enol> <ester>.** A further equivalent of D removes the much more acidic central <proton> of the new <1,3-diketone>, generating its extensively delocalized <enolate> and returning that second D equivalent to <pinacolone>. Aqueous <acid (chemistry)> workup protonates the product <enolate>; keto–<enol> equilibration gives the conjugated, internally stabilized by a <hydrogen bond> <enol> observed by <NMR>. The <acyl chloride> contributes its entire pivaloyl group; neither <tert-butyl group> is lost.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**One equivalent supplies <carbon>; the other acts as a <base (chemistry)> for the product.** The newly formed <1,3-diketone> is appreciably more acidic than the <ketone> from which D was prepared. <proton transfer> from its central CH2 to a second <enolate> equivalent is therefore favourable. Product-<enolate> stabilization also helps drive the acyl-substitution sequence towards completion.

The second <enolate> is consequently consumed as <base (chemistry)>, yielding <pinacolone>, rather than supplying a second <carbon> fragment to E. <acid (chemistry)> workup reverses the product <deprotonation>. Ignoring this <acid (chemistry)>–<base (chemistry)> step would predict that a single equivalent of D is sufficient to consume every equivalent of <acyl chloride>, despite the product immediately quenching part of the available <reagent>.

