= Solution
{parent=b}

Number the cyclopentenone <carbonyl> <carbon> $C_1$, its <alkene> <carbons> $C_2$ and $C_3$ with OR at $C_3$, and its saturated <carbons> $C_4$ and $C_5$. The <ester> <alkene> has alpha <carbon> $X$, bearing Me and CO2Et, and beta <carbon> $Y$, bearing the protected carbon substituent. **<lithium diisopropylamide> generates the ordinary <ketone> <enolate> at the saturated alpha <carbon> $C_5$.** The <acetonide> is retained.

The <carbon> end of this <enolate> attacks <ester> beta <carbon> Y. Its pi system shifts through X onto the <ester> <oxygen>, forming the first new bond $C_5{-}Y$ and an <ester> <enolate> at X. That <enolate> now attacks the original cyclopentenone beta <carbon> $C_3$ intramolecularly, giving the second new bond $X{-}C_3$. The original <enone> <electrons> move onto its <ketone> <oxygen>, and <protonation> of the final <ketone> <enolate> completes the product. The OR <substituent> is retained at the new bridgehead; it need not leave in either <Michael addition>.

The atom map provides a useful structural check. Between bridgeheads $C_3$ and $C_5$, the three paths are $C_3{-}C_2{-}C_1{-}C_5$, $C_3{-}C_4{-}C_5$, and $C_3{-}X{-}Y{-}C_5$. Their numbers of internal atoms are **2, 1 and 2**, establishing the <norbornane> skeleton. Both original <carbonyls> survive and both initial <alkenes> are consumed. The two successive <conjugate additions> give this atom map without requiring a concerted <cycloaddition>.

= c
{parent=B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**The new bridge is made by an intramolecular <Michael addition>.** On the <cyclohexanone> side, the methyl-bearing fused alpha <carbon> is quaternary and cannot be deprotonated there. <triethylamine> can instead generate a small equilibrium population of the <enolate> at the other, methylene alpha <carbon>. Its <carbon> attacks the beta <carbon> of the <enone> in the seven-membered ring. The <enone> pi <electrons> move towards its <carbonyl> and then onto <oxygen>, creating the new bridge and a second <enolate>.

<protonation> of that <enolate> at the former methyl-substituted <enone> alpha <carbon> restores the left-hand <ketone>. The initial <enone> C=C has therefore become a saturated C–C bond while one new transannular C–C bond forms. Both <ketones> are retained, and the initial two-ring framework becomes a three-ring framework without changing the total <index of hydrogen deficiency>. The methyl originally at the fused quaternary centre does not migrate; the other methyl ends up on a protonated, formerly unsaturated <carbon>. A strong-<base (chemistry)> concentration large enough for quantitative <enolate> generation is unnecessary for a favourable intramolecular trapping reaction, and neither an aldol <dehydration> nor a methyl shift is needed.

= d
{parent=B2}
