Number the OH-bearing <carbon> C1 and the adjacent brominated <carbon> C2. In the trans <bromohydrin>, <base (chemistry)> generates an <alkoxide>. A <chair conformation> can place O at C1 and Br at C2 trans-diaxial, aligning the <oxygen> <nucleophile> for <backside attack> at C2. Intramolecular SN2 closure expels <bromide> and inverts C2: **the retained C1–O bond and the newly formed C2–O bond are both on the original OH face**, giving the <epoxide> shown. <ring inversion> can provide the reactive chair even if it is not the dominant ground-state conformer.

The cis <bromohydrin> cannot place OH and Br trans-diaxial: one is axial when the other is equatorial. In the chair with Br axial, the <hydrogen> on C1 can instead be antiperiplanar to C2–Br. An <alkoxide>-assisted semipinacol pathway moves the C1–H <bonding pair> to C2 while the <oxygen> <lone pair> forms C1=O and <bromide> departs. **<hydride> migration gives the <ketone> without changing the <carbon> skeleton.** The <hydrogen> is transferred to C2 rather than expelled as a free <hydride>. The original phenyl-bearing stereocentre is not involved in either bond-reorganization sequence. Configuration controls which properly aligned displacement or migration can occur; a flat ring drawing alone conceals that distinction.

= b
{parent=B3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<protonation> of the secondary OH permits <water> departure. The neighbouring phenylthio <sulfur> can assist this departure by forming a bond to the adjacent <alcohol> <carbon>, generating a <thiiranium ion> spanning that <carbon> and the original <sulfur>-bearing <cyclohexane> <carbon>. Label the latter Q and the former L. **The temporary Q–S–L bridge transfers the phenylthio attachment from Q to L when its Q–S bond subsequently breaks.** <sulfur> attack opposite the departing OH accounts for the new C–S <stereochemistry> without requiring a freely rotating <carbocation>.

For the protected terminal <alcohol>, no free tethered OH is available to trap the bridge. Removal of a beta <hydrogen> from a <cyclohexane> <carbon> adjacent to Q forms the ring <alkene> while Q–S breaks. <sulfur> remains bonded to L, giving the allylic phenyl <thioether>. <acid (chemistry)> is regenerated by the <deprotonation>, so the <catalyst> need not be consumed.

For the free terminal <alcohol>, its <oxygen> attacks Q intramolecularly, opening Q–S instead. Count the new <ether> ring: **O–CH2–CH(Me)–L–Q**, a five-membered ring sharing Q with the <cyclohexane>. <deprotonation> gives the spirocyclic <ether>. This is capture of the same neighbouring-group-stabilized intermediate rather than migration of the <methyl group>. The pre-existing methyl-bearing <carbon> retains its configuration; its wedge/dash appearance changes when the product is redrawn in a different ring projection. The two substrates differ in whether a suitably placed <oxygen> <nucleophile> is available for intramolecular trapping.

\Image[paper-1-sulfur-pathways.png]
{title=Original local atom maps of sulfur participation, the equivalent paths of the bridged thietanium ion, and the five-membered intermediate in thiirane sulfur exchange}

= c
{parent=B3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Direct <iodide> attack at the terminal CH2–OMs would replace the <leaving group> without disturbing the adjacent stereocentre, so it cannot by itself explain racemization. **<sulfur> participation produces an intermediate with two equivalent methylene paths.** Label the original S-adjacent methylene A, the stereogenic <carbon> C, and its CH2–OMs <substituent> B. The <thioether> <sulfur> attacks B intramolecularly and expels <mesylate>, forming a four-membered thietanium circuit S–A–C–B–S. The rest of the original six-membered ring remains as the other path between S and C.

In this bridged <sulfonium ion>, A and B are both CH2 groups joining the same S and C centres. Exchanging them no longer changes the labelled molecular connectivity: the distinction that made C stereogenic has been removed. <iodide> can open the strained intermediate by <backside attack> at either A or B. Opening one S–CH2 bond gives one configuration of the six-membered product, while opening its equivalent partner exchanges which methylene is in the ring and which is the iodomethyl <substituent>, giving the opposite configuration.

**The <carbon> iodinated in each opening is a methylene; racemization appears at the adjacent <carbon> through exchange of equivalent paths.** It is not inversion at a CH2 stereocentre, nor does the <mesylate> <carbon> have to form a primary free <carbocation>. An <achiral> intermediate in an <achiral> medium gives equal mirror-related outcomes. <nitrogen> participation alone in a simple terminal displacement would not provide this specific equivalent-path explanation.

= d
{parent=B3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Distinguish the original <thiirane> <sulfur> $S_o$ from the <sulfur> $S_n$ supplied by <thiocyanate>. <sulfur> attack by $\mathrm{{}^-S_n{-}C\equiv N}$ at one <thiirane> <carbon> opens its C–$S_o$ bond with inversion. This produces a <thiolate> at the neighbouring <carbon> and a C–$S_n$–CN group at the attacked <carbon>. The OH-bearing remote <carbon> is unaffected.

The original <thiolate> $S_o^-$ now attacks the nitrile <carbon> intramolecularly. Shift one C≡N pi pair onto <nitrogen>. The resulting cyclic imidodithiocarbonate has the five-membered sequence **ring <carbon>–$S_o$–C(=N$^-$)–$S_n$–adjacent ring <carbon>**; its two <carbon> atoms are also part of the original six-membered carbocycle, exactly giving the hinted bicyclic intermediate.

Collapse can reform C≡N while breaking the nitrile-<carbon>–$S_n$ bond. This transfers the CN group to $S_o$ and generates $S_n^-$ as the <thiolate> on the other ring <carbon>. That <thiolate> attacks the remaining C–$S_o$ bond from the back, expelling $\mathrm{{}^-S_o{-}C\equiv N}$ and reclosing the <thiirane>. **Each <thiirane> <carbon> has undergone one inversion, so the new <sulfur> bridge lies on the opposite face; the OH configuration is retained.** The <thiocyanate> <sulfur> has entered the ring and the original ring <sulfur> has left as <thiocyanate>. This atom-labelled exchange mechanism explains how the same elemental formula can conceal <sulfur> replacement; <isotope> labelling would be needed to test that exchange experimentally. A single <epimerization> at the OH <carbon> would give the wrong <stereochemical> outcome.

