**A is the predicted major <diastereomer>.** At the <carbon> adjacent to the <ketone>, take phenyl as the large group, vinyl as medium, and <hydrogen> as small. There is no adjacent heteroatom donor that would force a five-membered chelate. The appropriate first model is therefore the non-chelating <Felkin-Anh model>.

View along the <carbonyl>-<carbon>–stereocentre bond. Rotate to place the large alpha <phenyl group> approximately perpendicular to the <carbonyl> plane. Of the two resulting approaches opposite that large group, the favourable <Bürgi-Dunitz trajectory> passes nearer <hydrogen> rather than vinyl. Propyl transfer to the <carbonyl> and <protonation> of its <oxygen> give the configuration labelled A: in the printed projection the new OH projects forward and the propyl bond backward, while the original vinyl bond remains backward. Rotation used to construct a <Newman projection> must not be mistaken for inversion of the original stereocentre.

This is a relative-<selectivity> prediction, not a numerical product ratio. Simple attack on the face that happens to look less crowded in the initial flat drawing is unreliable because the relevant C–C single bond can rotate. B is the competing product from the less favourable approach beside vinyl. The model and its preference for the trajectory beside the small group are described in the institutional treatment.<Felkin-Anh and Cram Chelate by University of Windsor>[pp. 1–2, section 1, nonpolar alpha substituents and the small-group trajectory]{ref}

\Image[paper-1-felkin-model.png]
{title=Original Newman construction of the preferred Felkin–Anh approach and the local stereochemistry corresponding to product A}

= b
{parent=B4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <alkene> attacks <electrophilic> <mercury (element)>(II), with <acetate> departure as appropriate, to give a bridged <mercurinium ion> between the ring <carbon> and the exocyclic methylene. That bridge is polarized: the more substituted ring <carbon> can better stabilize developing positive charge than the terminal CH2 <carbon>. **<water> attacks the ring <carbon>**, opening the <mercury (element)> bridge there, and <deprotonation> gives the tertiary <alcohol> with an exocyclic CH2–HgOAc <substituent>.

Subsequent <sodium>-borohydride <demercuration> replaces the terminal C–Hg bond by C–H. Thus the original exocyclic CH2 becomes **Me**, and the new OH remains on the substituted ring <carbon>. The result is Markovnikov hydration of the exocyclic <alkene>. There is no need for a freely equilibrating tertiary <carbocation>, and no rearrangement of the <carbohydrate> <carbon> skeleton or cleavage of the methyl <acetal> is required. The question does not require the <mercury (element)>-stage facial selection or a detailed <demercuration> mechanism.

= c
{parent=B4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

<protonation> activates the <oxygen> of the anomeric <acetal> bridge. Cleavage of its bond to the <anomeric carbon> releases that tether <oxygen> as the terminal primary <alcohol>, while the other ring <oxygen> stabilizes the developing anomeric <electrophile> by <lone pair> donation. The resulting <oxocarbenium> description has a nearly planar reactive anomeric centre; it need not be drawn as a long-lived free <carbocation>.

<methanol> attacks that centre, giving a protonated methyl <acetal>, and <deprotonation> regenerates <acid (chemistry)>. **The former bridge becomes CH2OH; the anomeric <substituent> becomes OMe.** The other C–O bonds and existing <carbon> configurations are retained. The ring can adopt a different chair once the bridge constraint is removed, so an apparent movement of <substituents> in the projection does not imply inversion at all the other <carbons>.

The depicted anomeric OMe is axial in the product chair. This geometry allows an endocyclic <oxygen> <lone pair> to align antiperiplanar to the exocyclic C–OMe bond, providing $n_O\rightarrow\sigma^*_{C-O}$ stabilization associated with the <anomeric effect>. Acidic conditions also permit cleavage and recapture at the anomeric centre, so selection is not simply backside displacement at an unchanged tetrahedral starting <carbon>. The <anomeric effect> rationalizes the specified axial product; the scheme alone gives neither its numerical equilibrium population nor evidence that every other <anomer> is absent.

= d
{parent=B4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

<triethylamine> removes an alpha <proton> from <nitromethane> to generate a <nitronate> population. Its <carbon> attacks the cyclic <ketone> <carbonyl> of E, forming a new C–CH2NO2 bond while the C=O pi pair moves onto <oxygen>. <protonation> by triethylammonium or another <proton> donor gives the tertiary <alcohol> F and regenerates <base (chemistry)>. **This is a <Henry reaction>, also called a <nitroaldol reaction>:** the former <ketone> <carbon> now bears both OH and CH2NO2.

The <nitro group> remains attached to its original <nitromethane> <carbon>; attack through <oxygen> would give a different connectivity. The methyl <acetal> and the original <alcohol> <substituents> need not be cleaved or displaced. This addition produces the isolated connectivity without requiring <dehydration> to a <nitroalkene>. The stated complex mixture is compatible with competing additions or <stereoisomers>, but the scheme supplies no basis for claiming F is the unique or kinetically dominant product. Only its formation is being explained.

